// prism_form (app/, Tier 2) -- `form_of` derived forms and autosave/draft recovery (#87). This is
// the STATE layer above prism/ui/prism_ui_form.nova (MB.1), which already owns the node-tree /
// typed-field / validation-rule vocabulary (PrismFormFieldKind's 7 kinds, PrismFormRule's 3 rules,
// PrismForm, PrismFormField) and rendering. Nothing in that vocabulary is reimplemented here --
// this file never invents a field kind, a validation rule, or a rendered node; it only manages
// WHICH VALUES a PrismForm currently holds, whether that differs from what was last saved, and how
// an in-progress edit survives a reload. Matches prism_route.nova's own layering stance (pure
// data/logic over Result, no rendering, no owned timers) -- read that file first; this one copies
// its shape deliberately.
//
// ── `form_of` HERE IS A LIBRARY FUNCTION, NOT THE DSL KEYWORD PRISM_SPEC.md SKETCHES ───────────
// PRISM_SPEC.md Part VI example 21 and PRISM_UNIVERSAL_UI_PLAN.md sketch `form_of e` as something
// the compiler recognizes inside a `face` body, reactively re-deriving UI as `e`'s fields change --
// that requires `face`/`wire` compiler support this codebase does not have yet (same gap
// prism_ui_form.nova's own header already accepts: it composes the 22-primitive vocabulary by hand
// because there is no DSL to lower). `prism_fm_form_of` below is the same kind of stand-in: an
// ordinary function, called once, that reads a struct value through NOVA's RTTI keystone
// (`field_names`/`field_types`/`field_get`, PRISM_SPEC.md's own words for this) and returns a
// PrismForm. It has no way to re-run itself when `e` changes later -- the CALLER re-derives (calls
// it again) whenever that is wanted, exactly as re-rendering is the caller's job everywhere else in
// this codebase's current (non-reactive) rendering story.
//
// ── DESIGN DECISION: what "cannot silently miss a field" CAN and CANNOT mean without `face` ─────
// The brief for this file asks for a form "derived from a typed shape... so adding a field to the
// type cannot leave the form silently missing it." Without compiler-level reactivity, the honest,
// checkable version of that guarantee is: `prism_fm_form_of` enumerates literally every name
// `field_names(e)` reports for the value passed to it, AT THE MOMENT IT IS CALLED -- there is no
// hand-maintained field list anywhere in this file for a new struct field to fall out of sync with
// (contrast a hand-built `prism_ui_form.prism_form([prism_form_text("a",...), prism_form_text("b",
// ...)])`, where adding field `c` to the struct is silently invisible to that list until a human
// remembers to edit it). Concretely: if a field cannot be represented, `prism_fm_form_of` returns
// `Result::Err` naming that field -- it NEVER drops a field from the derived form silently. That is
// the guarantee this file can make. What it CANNOT make structural: (1) that `prism_fm_form_of` is
// actually CALLED again after the struct's shape changes -- a caller who cached an old PrismFmState
// across a recompile is a caller bug this layer cannot see, same as any cached derived value in any
// language; (2) that EVERY field type is representable at all -- see the next design decision.
//
// ── DESIGN DECISION: the derivable vocabulary is `int`/`bool`/`string` ONLY, and it FAILS LOUDLY
// on everything else, rather than guessing ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// `field_types(e)` returns the RAW, unnormalized declared annotation for each field (verified: an
// `age: int` field reports literally "int"; a WIDTH-annotated `age: i32` reports literally "i32",
// not "int" -- see probe below). This file recognizes exactly the three bare annotations
// prism_ui_form.nova's closed 7-kind vocabulary can represent from a scalar with no caller-supplied
// option list: `int` -> Number, `bool` -> Toggle, `string` -> Text/Email/Password (see next
// decision for which). Anything else -- `i32`/`u8`/`f64` (an explicit width; silently widening or
// narrowing it back through a PrismFormField's own int would reintroduce exactly the implicit-width
// conversion bug LOCK-4 made a compile error elsewhere in this language), `float` (prism_ui_form.nova
// has no float-valued field kind to map it to -- that gap belongs to that file, not a reason to fake
// one here), `T?`/`Option<T>`, a nested struct, an enum, or a list -- makes `prism_fm_form_of` return
// `Result::Err` naming the exact field and its declared type, rather than silently omitting the
// field (which would violate the design decision above) or silently misrendering it. The caller's
// escape hatch is `prism_fm_from_form`: hand-build the awkward field with prism_ui_form's own
// constructors, splice it into the list `prism_fm_form_of` would have produced for the rest, and
// wrap the result. This is the same "derive what is safe, name what is not, let the caller finish
// it by hand" posture prism_secret.nova takes for `@redact` crossing a module boundary.
//
// ── DESIGN DECISION: which STRING sub-kind (text/email/password) is a NAMING CONVENTION, stated
// as exactly that -- not a guarantee, because NOVA cannot give one here ─────────────────────────
// A struct field's declared type for an email address, a password, and a plain string are all the
// literal same annotation: `string`. There is no nominal `Email`/`Password` type in this codebase
// for `field_types` to report, and no compiler-visible marker (an `@redact`-tagged field's mask is
// NOT reflectable -- see the SECRETS decision below) that would let this be derived rather than
// guessed. `_prism_fm_derive_kind` therefore inspects the FIELD NAME (case-insensitively) for
// `password`/`passwd`/`secret`/`token` -> Password, then `email` -> Email, else Text. STATE THIS
// PLAINLY: this is a CONVENTION, not a structural guarantee. A secret-holding field named outside
// that list (`credential`, `pin`, `apiKey`) becomes an ordinary Text field and WOULD be eligible for
// autosave. This is exactly the failure mode the SECRETS decision below refuses to leave resting on
// a naming guess -- see there for what IS structural.
//
// ── ⛔ SECRETS: what is STRUCTURAL vs. CONVENTIONAL here (read before trusting draft persistence)
// The brief: "a password field must never reach a draft store... this layer must make it
// UNREPRESENTABLE, not merely discouraged." The naming heuristic above cannot deliver that (a false
// negative is silent and real). What DOES: `_prism_fm_field_raw` is the ONE function in this file
// that ever turns a `PrismFormField`'s value into a string that could leave this module (draft
// snapshot, dirty comparison, submit). It pattern-matches prism_ui_form's own closed field-kind
// vocabulary and tags every `password`-kind field's raw value `fm_raw_secret = true`.
// `_prism_fm_encode_values(form, include_secret)` is the ONLY place that string is either kept or
// dropped, and `prism_fm_draft_snapshot` is the ONLY caller that passes `include_secret = false`.
// STRUCTURAL: once a field carries `PrismFormFieldKind.FormFieldPassword` -- however it got that
// kind, `prism_fm_form_of`'s naming guess OR a caller's own explicit
// `prism_ui_form.prism_form_password(...)` -- there is no code path in `prism_fm_draft_snapshot`
// that can emit its value: the exclusion happens at the single choke point every persisted string
// passes through, not at each call site (nothing to forget to add at a second call site, because
// there is only one). CONVENTIONAL (this file cannot close it, stated plainly, same posture as
// prism_secret.nova's own header): (1) whether a given field ends up AS a Password-kind field at
// all, when it was derived rather than hand-built, is the naming guess above; (2) `prism_fm_submit`
// DELIBERATELY returns the real password value (a caller must be able to send the real password to
// the server) -- that is the "explicit, deliberate unwrap" this module's one legitimate exit for a
// secret value, mirroring `prism_secret.sec_unwrap`, and it is never persisted by this file, only
// returned to the caller's own memory; (3) nothing stops a caller from taking that returned value
// and writing it to disk themselves -- no library can prevent that, only make the ACCIDENTAL path
// (autosave) provably not do it.
//
// ── DESIGN DECISION: autosave (permissive) and submit (strict) do not share a code path ─────────
// `prism_fm_draft_snapshot` never validates -- a half-typed email must be saveable as a draft, or
// autosave is useless for exactly the input it exists to protect. `prism_fm_submit` always
// validates (delegates to prism_ui_form.prism_form_validate, the SAME rules the rendered form shows
// errors for -- not a second, divergent rule set) and returns Err naming every violated field before
// returning any value. These are two different public functions with two different Result shapes on
// purpose: a boolean "strict: bool" flag on one shared function is exactly the kind of thing
// CLAUDE.md warns a NOVA bool cannot be trusted to carry reliably, and more importantly would put
// the "did this validate" decision behind a call-site argument instead of the function's own name.
//
// ── DESIGN DECISION: dirty is measured from a FINGERPRINT of the SAVED baseline, not a clock ─────
// A draft is only worth surfacing if it is DIFFERENT from what is currently true -- "recover draft?"
// on a form nobody touched is noise. Two things must be told apart, and they are NOT the same
// check: (a) is the CURRENT in-memory form dirty vs. its OWN last-saved baseline (`prism_fm_is_dirty`
// -- an ordinary "unsaved changes" indicator); (b) does a DRAFT PULLED BACK FROM STORAGE, possibly
// written in a previous session, actually change anything relative to the record's CURRENT saved
// state (`prism_fm_draft_inspect`'s `FmDraftNone` vs. `FmDraftRecoverable`/`FmDraftConflicting`).
// (b) additionally needs to detect the CONFLICT case: the record moved on the server while the
// draft sat in storage, so blindly applying the draft would silently clobber a newer save with
// stale data. Nothing in this pure, backend-agnostic module can read a server clock or an ETag the
// caller's own record type may or may not have -- so `_prism_fm_fingerprint` derives a version
// marker the only way available here: `sha256` of the canonical (non-secret) field values at the
// moment the current baseline was captured (`prism_fm_form_of` / `prism_fm_mark_saved`). Two
// baselines with identical non-secret content always fingerprint identically (content-addressed, no
// clock skew, no caller-supplied ID required) and two different baselines collide only as often as
// SHA-256 does. A draft's embedded fingerprint disagreeing with the CURRENT baseline's fingerprint
// is exactly "the record changed since this draft was taken" -- `FmDraftConflicting`. Precedence
// is deliberate: a draft that changes NOTHING is reported `FmDraftNone` even if the fingerprints
// differ (see `prism_fm_draft_inspect`) -- a no-op recovery is not worth a conflict prompt either.
//
// ── DESIGN DECISION: this module never touches storage -- same stance as prism_route's scroll ───
// `prism_fm_draft_snapshot` returns an opaque string; `prism_fm_draft_inspect`/`_apply` accept one
// back. Nothing here calls a filesystem or `localStorage` API. Exactly like prism_route's own
// scroll memory ("the router cannot read a live DOM scroll position... saving is always an explicit
// caller action"), THIS module cannot know whether its host is a browser tab, a native shell, or a
// test harness, and guessing would make it untestable outside whichever one it guessed. The caller
// (the platform-specific glue, or Forge on the server) decides WHERE the string goes and WHEN
// autosave fires (a debounce timer, a blur handler, a periodic tick) -- this module answers "is
// there anything worth saving" (`prism_fm_is_dirty`) and "what would recovering this actually do"
// (`prism_fm_draft_inspect`), never "save this now."
//
// ── DESIGN DECISION: editing goes through ONE raw-STRING setter, mirroring the DOM's own model ───
// `prism_fm_set_field(state, name, raw: string)` interprets `raw` according to the field's CURRENT
// declared kind (an int field parses with `parse_int_safe`, a toggle recognizes "true"/"1" and
// "false"/"0", everything else passes through) rather than exposing five kind-specific setters. A
// browser `<input>`/`<select>` change event hands a caller a string regardless of the field's
// semantic kind (even `<input type=number>`); this mirrors that reality instead of fighting it, and
// it is the same "the KIND governs how untyped input is interpreted, never a caller's guess"
// posture prism_route.nova takes for a route's typed segment. Draft recovery reuses this exact
// function per field -- there is no second, parallel "apply a recovered value" code path to drift
// from the one a live UI event would call.
//
// ── ⛔ MEASURED THIS SESSION: the `bool(x)` cast builtin CRASHES on a reflected `any` value ───────
// Needed to know, for `prism_fm_form_of`, whether a `bool`-typed struct field read back via
// `field_get` (which returns `any`) could be safely turned into a real `bool`. Three things were
// measured with a minimal probe (a 3-field struct, `field_get` on each field):
//   1. `field_get(e, boolField)` passed DIRECTLY as the argument to a function whose parameter is
//      statically typed `bool` (no cast) works correctly -- prints "true"/"false" as expected. Same
//      result for a `string` field into a `string` parameter (verified with a non-ASCII value,
//      "héllo", byte length round-tripped correctly) and an `int` field into an `int` parameter.
//   2. `str()` on the RAW reflected `any` bool (before it crosses any typed parameter) prints "1"/
//      "0" -- NOT "true"/"false". The SAME boolean value, once passed through a statically `bool`-
//      typed parameter and read back, stringifies as "true"/"false". This is a second, concrete
//      confirmation of the already-recorded "a NOVA bool changes representation across a call
//      boundary" gotcha, not a new one -- recorded here because it directly shaped this file's
//      design: NEVER compare a bool's string form across that boundary; convert with `true`/`false`
//      LITERALS in this file's own code (see `_prism_fm_field_raw`), never by inspecting `str()` of
//      a reflected value.
//   3. `bool(field_get(e, boolField))` -- the explicit `bool(x)` CAST builtin, applied to that same
//      reflected `any` -- CRASHES the process (exit code -2147483645 / 0x80000003, STATUS_
//      BREAKPOINT; no NOVA-level error, no stderr). `int(field_get(e, intField))`, by contrast,
//      returned 42 correctly for the same probe. This is a genuine NOVA defect (`bool()`'s cast path
//      is unimplemented or mis-lowered for a boxed/any operand while `int()`'s is not) -- tracked as
//      a compiler defect, NOT fixed here (`prism/` is this session's scope). CONSEQUENCE: this file
//      never calls the `bool(x)`/`float(x)` cast builtins on a reflected value anywhere --
//      `_prism_fm_build_field` passes `field_get(e, name)` DIRECTLY into prism_ui_form's
//      already-`bool`-typed `prism_form_toggle` parameter (measured safe, point 1 above), and never
//      calls `bool()` at all.
//
// PUBLIC API
//   Construction
//     prism_fm_form_of<T>(e: T)                RTTI-derived, see design decisions -> Result<PrismFmState>
//     prism_fm_from_form(form: PrismForm)       wrap an explicitly hand-built form -> Result<PrismFmState>
//   Editing (immutable -- every op returns a NEW PrismFmState; `fm_st_saved` never changes here)
//     prism_fm_set_field(state, name, raw: string)  -> Result<PrismFmState>
//   Saved-baseline transition
//     prism_fm_mark_saved(state)                -> Result<PrismFmState>   current values become the new baseline
//   Accessors
//     prism_fm_current_form(state) -> PrismForm    prism_fm_saved_form(state) -> PrismForm
//     prism_fm_version(state) -> string             (opaque content fingerprint of the saved baseline)
//   Dirty tracking
//     prism_fm_is_dirty(state)                  -> Result<bool>
//   Submit -- total: validated values, or every reason it cannot submit, never both, never neither
//     prism_fm_submit(state)                    -> Result<dict>   name -> raw string, INCLUDES secrets
//   Draft / autosave (#87) -- this module never touches storage, see design decision
//     prism_fm_draft_snapshot(state)                            -> Result<string>   secrets OMITTED
//     prism_fm_draft_inspect(fresh_state, snapshot)             -> Result<PrismFmDraftInfo>
//     prism_fm_draft_apply(fresh_state, snapshot, allow_conflict: bool) -> Result<PrismFmState>
//     prism_fm_should_offer_recovery(info)                      -> bool
//     PrismFmDraftStatus                        FmDraftNone() | FmDraftRecoverable() | FmDraftConflicting()
//     prism_fm_draft_status(info) -> PrismFmDraftStatus     prism_fm_draft_status_str(info) -> string
//     prism_fm_draft_changed_fields(info)       -> list     field names the draft would actually change
//
// ── ⛔ MEASURED THIS SESSION: a module can export at most ~32 top-level declarations to an
// EXTERNAL caller before the rest become silently unreachable -- NOT a soundness bug (nothing
// mis-executes), but a real, previously-undocumented ceiling this file is written to respect ──────
// While wiring this file's own KAT, several LATER top-level functions (originally: prism_fm_
// draft_apply onward) failed to resolve from the *importing* file -- "module 'prism_form' has no
// exported function 'X'" (qualified) / "unknown identifier 'X'" (unqualified) -- even though X was
// declared, correctly typed, never inside a lambda/generic-only path, and earlier functions in the
// SAME file resolved fine. Isolated with a minimal two-file repro (a module of N trivial
// `fn gc_fNN(s: GcS) -> Result<int>` declarations, imported and called from a second file): the
// LAST declaration in the module resolves when the module has <=32 total top-level declarations
// (fn + type + enum, counted together) and FAILS to resolve at 33, reproducibly, independent of the
// function's name, arity, parameter types (tried bool/int/struct/enum-carrying-struct), generic-ness,
// and position relative to a caller's own nesting depth (ruled out separately). Renaming the
// function does not help; splitting the caller into shallower functions does not help; the count is
// over the WHOLE module file, not per section. This file was originally written at 39 total
// declarations (draft_apply was #35) and hit exactly this wall -- see git history / the session
// report for the full before/after. FIX APPLIED HERE: five single-call-site private helpers
// (`_prism_fm_apply_number/_range/_toggle`, `_prism_fm_dicts_differ`, `_prism_fm_diff_against`,
// `_prism_fm_status_is_conflicting`, and two named-constant functions replaced by inline literals)
// were folded into their one caller, bringing the file to 31 total declarations -- comfortably under
// the observed ceiling, confirmed by the KAT (which exercises every public function through a real
// cross-module import) compiling and passing. CONSEQUENCE FOR FUTURE EDITS: adding a new public
// function to this file's small remaining margin is fine; adding several, or restoring any of the
// folded helpers as separate functions, must be re-checked against this ceiling (recompile the KAT
// and confirm every function it calls still resolves) before assuming the addition is safe. Tracked
// as a compiler defect (a fixed-size table or similar in the cross-module export/import path), NOT
// fixed here -- `prism/` is this session's scope, `nova_compiler.nova` is not.

import prism_ui_form

// ── PrismFmDraftStatus: closed, 3-outcome. See DESIGN DECISION above for the precedence (a no-op
// draft is FmDraftNone even when its fingerprint disagrees with the current baseline -- "would this
// recovery change anything" outranks "did the record move"). No public constructors: unlike
// RtParamKind/RtNavKind in prism_route.nova (which a CALLER must construct to pass in), a caller of
// this file only ever RECEIVES a PrismFmDraftStatus (from `prism_fm_draft_status`) and matches on
// it -- there is no legitimate call site that needs to build one from outside this file.
enum PrismFmDraftStatus
    FmDraftNone()
    FmDraftRecoverable()
    FmDraftConflicting()

// ── Value types. Every field is `fm_`-prefixed with a per-struct sub-tag (`fm_st_`, `fm_di_`,
// `fm_raw_`), mirroring prism_route.nova's `rt_route_*` / `rt_match_*` convention under its shared
// `rt_` module prefix -- NOVA's struct field->slot map is GLOBAL, so a bare `form`/`version`/`name`
// here would risk colliding with an unrelated field of that name anywhere else in the program.

// The state this module manages: CURRENT live values (`fm_st_form`, what the user has typed so
// far -- may be invalid, may be dirty), the last-known SAVED baseline (`fm_st_saved` -- what the
// record actually is, as of the last `prism_fm_form_of`/`prism_fm_mark_saved`), and a content
// fingerprint of that baseline (`fm_st_version` -- see DESIGN DECISION on conflict detection). The
// two PrismForm values always share the same field SET (same names, same kinds) -- this layer edits
// VALUES, it never adds or removes a field from a form under management.
type PrismFmState
    fm_st_form: PrismForm
    fm_st_saved: PrismForm
    fm_st_version: string

// The outcome of inspecting a persisted draft against the CURRENT saved baseline -- see
// `prism_fm_draft_inspect`. `fm_di_changed_fields` is empty exactly when the status is
// `FmDraftNone`, and lists every field NAME (in the draft's own encoded order, itself the source
// form's field-declaration order) whose draft value differs from the field's current saved value.
type PrismFmDraftInfo
    fm_di_status: PrismFmDraftStatus
    fm_di_changed_fields: list

// Internal plumbing only (never appears in a public signature): one field's value, reduced to the
// uniform raw-string representation every comparison/snapshot/submit operation in this file works
// over, plus whether reading it out at all requires the caller's explicit consent (secret-kind
// fields). See the SECRETS design decision above -- `fm_raw_secret` is the ONE flag this whole
// file's password-exclusion logic is built on.
type PrismFmRaw
    fm_raw_name: string
    fm_raw_value: string
    fm_raw_secret: bool

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ── Field name -> label, and field type -> derived kind (form_of's naming convention) ───────────

// "emp_first_name" -> "Emp First Name": split on '_', capitalize each piece, join with a space.
// Mechanical and honest about it -- see the form_of design decisions above for why this does NOT
// try to strip a struct's own field-prefix convention (this file has no way to know what that
// convention is). A caller who wants a nicer label replaces the derived field with
// `prism_ui_form.prism_form_field_with_help`-style hand construction; nothing here hides that this
// is a mechanical transform, not a real "display name" derivation.
fn _prism_fm_humanize(fname: string) -> string
    let words = split(fname, "_")
    let out = []
    for w in words
        if len(w) > 0
            push(out, upper(slice(w, 0, 1)) + slice(w, 1, len(w)))
    join(out, " ")

// See DESIGN DECISION above: "" means unsupported (form_of fails loudly on that field), and the
// string/email/password split is a NAMING CONVENTION, not a structural guarantee.
fn _prism_fm_derive_kind(fname: string, ftype: string) -> string
    if ftype == "int"
        return "number"
    if ftype == "bool"
        return "toggle"
    if ftype == "string"
        let lname = lower(fname)
        if find(lname, "password") >= 0 or find(lname, "passwd") >= 0 or find(lname, "secret") >= 0 or find(lname, "token") >= 0
            return "password"
        if find(lname, "email") >= 0
            return "email"
        return "text"
    ""

// Builds ONE derived field. `field_get(e, fname)` is passed DIRECTLY into prism_ui_form's own
// typed constructor parameters (`value: int`/`value: bool`/`value: string`) with no cast builtin in
// between -- see the "MEASURED THIS SESSION" header note for why that is the safe path and `bool()`
// is not.
//
// The literal i32 bounds on the Number branch are the conventional, documented default for a
// form_of-derived Number field (NOT extracted to a named constant -- see the file-wide declaration-
// count note below the PUBLIC API block for why every private helper here is judged against a real
// budget, not free). Reflection has no way to know the field's real business range (an age vs. a
// cent amount vs. a year) -- these are simply wide enough that the derived field's OWN [min,max]
// never itself rejects a value at construction (prism_form_number only enforces min<=max there; an
// out-of-[lo,hi] VALUE surfaces later, at `prism_form_validate` time). i32, not i64, is itself a
// deliberate choice: wide enough for the overwhelming majority of real fields while still catching
// the common "this looks like a raw i64 timestamp/id, not a form input" case with a validate-time
// message instead of accepting silently. A caller with a field outside this range replaces it with
// a properly-bounded `prism_ui_form.prism_form_number(...)` call (see `prism_fm_from_form`).
fn <T> _prism_fm_build_field(e: T, fname: string, ftype: string) -> Result<PrismFormField>
    let kind = _prism_fm_derive_kind(fname, ftype)
    let label = _prism_fm_humanize(fname)
    if kind == "number"
        return prism_ui_form.prism_form_number(fname, label, field_get(e, fname), -2147483648, 2147483647)
    if kind == "toggle"
        return prism_ui_form.prism_form_toggle(fname, label, field_get(e, fname))
    if kind == "password"
        return prism_ui_form.prism_form_password(fname, label, field_get(e, fname))
    if kind == "email"
        return prism_ui_form.prism_form_email(fname, label, field_get(e, fname))
    if kind == "text"
        return prism_ui_form.prism_form_text(fname, label, field_get(e, fname))
    err("prism_fm_form_of: field '{fname}' has type '{ftype}', which form_of does not derive a form field for (supported: int, bool, string -- see header) -- build it explicitly via prism_ui_form and splice it into the derived field list, then wrap with prism_fm_from_form")

// ── Construction ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

fn _prism_fm_state_new(form: PrismForm) -> Result<PrismFmState>
    let version = _prism_fm_fingerprint(form)?
    ok(PrismFmState(form, form, version))

// See the `form_of` design decisions above for exactly what this does and does not guarantee.
fn <T> prism_fm_form_of(e: T) -> Result<PrismFmState>
    let names = field_names(e)
    let types = field_types(e)
    let fields = []
    let idx = [0]
    for fname in names
        let i = idx[0]
        idx[0] = i + 1
        let f = _prism_fm_build_field(e, fname, types[i])?
        push(fields, f)
    let form = prism_ui_form.prism_form(fields)?
    _prism_fm_state_new(form)

// Wraps an explicitly hand-built PrismForm (any mix of prism_ui_form's 7 field kinds, including
// Range/Choice which form_of never derives) so it gets the SAME editing/dirty/draft/submit
// machinery as a derived one. This is the escape hatch every design decision above points to when
// form_of's naming convention or scalar-only vocabulary is not enough for a real field.
fn prism_fm_from_form(form: PrismForm) -> Result<PrismFmState>
    _prism_fm_state_new(form)

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ── The single raw-value choke point (see SECRETS design decision) ─────────────────────────────

// The ONE function in this file that reads a value out of a PrismFormField. Every downstream
// consumer (dirty check, fingerprint, draft snapshot, submit) goes through this, which is what
// makes the password exclusion in `_prism_fm_encode_values` a single, auditable choke point instead
// of a rule every call site has to remember separately. Exhaustive over prism_ui_form's 7 kind tags
// via string comparison (not a compiler-checked `match`, because `PrismFormFieldKind` is declared
// in a different file and this file only sees its kind as a string via
// `prism_form_field_kind_str` -- see that function's own doc for why matching its ENUM directly
// from here is deliberately avoided). The final `err` arm is the substitute for compiler
// exhaustiveness: prism_ui_form.nova growing an 8th kind fails LOUDLY here on first use rather than
// silently dropping or misreading that field's value.
fn _prism_fm_field_raw(field: PrismFormField) -> Result<PrismFmRaw>
    let fname = prism_ui_form.prism_form_field_name(field)
    let kind = prism_ui_form.prism_form_field_kind_str(field)
    if kind == "text" or kind == "email"
        return ok(PrismFmRaw(fname, prism_ui_form.prism_form_field_text_value(field, ""), false))
    if kind == "password"
        return ok(PrismFmRaw(fname, prism_ui_form.prism_form_field_text_value(field, ""), true))
    if kind == "number"
        return ok(PrismFmRaw(fname, str(prism_ui_form.prism_form_field_number_value(field, 0)), false))
    if kind == "range"
        return ok(PrismFmRaw(fname, str(prism_ui_form.prism_form_field_range_value(field, 0)), false))
    if kind == "choice"
        return ok(PrismFmRaw(fname, prism_ui_form.prism_form_field_choice_selected(field, ""), false))
    if kind == "toggle"
        let b = prism_ui_form.prism_form_field_bool_value(field, false)
        // ⛔ The `if` MUST sit on the same line as the `=`. Written as `let s =` followed by a
        // newline and an indented `if`, this compiles HERE (inside an `if` block) but is an
        // E0001 "unexpected NEWLINE in expression" at function-body level -- and where it does
        // compile it yields a value that CORRUPTS the struct built from it: reading
        // `raw.fm_raw_secret` off the resulting PrismFmRaw faulted with an access violation.
        // Measured 2026-09-04; that crash is what this comment exists to prevent recurring.
        let s = if b
            "true"
        else
            "false"
        return ok(PrismFmRaw(fname, s, false))
    err("prism_form: field '{fname}' has an unrecognized kind '{kind}' -- prism_ui_form.nova's field-kind vocabulary grew since this file was written; add a case to _prism_fm_field_raw before trusting draft/dirty/submit on this field")

// `include_secret = false` is the ONLY mode anything persisted (prism_fm_draft_snapshot,
// _prism_fm_fingerprint) is built from. `include_secret = true` is used ONLY for in-memory
// comparisons (`prism_fm_is_dirty`) and the deliberate-unwrap `prism_fm_submit` -- see SECRETS.
// ⛔ `include_secret` is an INT 0/1, NOT a bool. A NOVA bool changes representation across a CALL
// BOUNDARY (inline int vs fn-arg bool), and passing `true` here faulted with an access violation
// -- measured 2026-09-04, and the reason prism_event/prism_media/prism_notice all use int 0/1 for
// their own flags. The parameter is the boundary, so this is where the rule has to be applied.
fn _prism_fm_encode_values(form: PrismForm, include_secret: int) -> Result<dict>
    let out = {}
    for field in prism_ui_form.prism_form_fields(form)
        let raw = _prism_fm_field_raw(field)?
        let keep = include_secret == 1 or not raw.fm_raw_secret
        if keep
            out[raw.fm_raw_name] = raw.fm_raw_value
    ok(out)

// Deterministic text for hashing/persisting: JSON object, keys in the FORM's OWN field-declaration
// order (not merely dict insertion order, though NOVA dicts do preserve that too -- iterating the
// form's own field list ties this ordering to something a human editing/diffing two snapshots would
// recognize, and stays correct independent of dict implementation details). Hand-built via
// `json_stringify` + `+`, never a generic `to_json()` -- see the file-wide ban on calling generic
// serialization on an imported struct (prism_secret.nova's header measured why).
fn _prism_fm_canonical_json(vals: dict, form: PrismForm) -> string
    let parts = []
    for field in prism_ui_form.prism_form_fields(form)
        let fname = prism_ui_form.prism_form_field_name(field)
        if fname in vals
            push(parts, json_stringify(fname) + ":" + json_stringify(vals[fname]))
    "\{" + join(parts, ",") + "\}"

// See DESIGN DECISION "dirty is measured from a fingerprint of the saved baseline".
fn _prism_fm_fingerprint(form: PrismForm) -> Result<string>
    let vals = _prism_fm_encode_values(form, 0)?
    ok(sha256(_prism_fm_canonical_json(vals, form)))

// ── Editing ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

fn _prism_fm_parse_bool(raw: string) -> Result<bool>
    if raw == "true" or raw == "1"
        return ok(true)
    if raw == "false" or raw == "0"
        return ok(false)
    err("prism_form: '{raw}' is not a valid boolean (expected true/false or 1/0)")

// Rebuilds ONE field with a new raw value, per its CURRENT kind (see DESIGN DECISION on the
// raw-string setter), then reattaches its EXISTING rules and help text (prism_ui_form's
// constructors reset both to empty/none -- this function is the only place in this file that must
// remember to carry them forward, so it does it once, here). Number/Range inline their own
// `parse_int_safe(raw)?` and Toggle its own `_prism_fm_parse_bool(raw)?` directly as a call
// argument (an ordinary expression position -- the "`?` cannot appear inside a LAMBDA" restriction
// does not apply to an if-expression branch) rather than each getting its own one-call-site helper
// function -- see the file-wide declaration-count note below the PUBLIC API block for why.
fn _prism_fm_apply_raw(field: PrismFormField, raw: string) -> Result<PrismFormField>
    let name = prism_ui_form.prism_form_field_name(field)
    let label = prism_ui_form.prism_form_field_label(field)
    let kind = prism_ui_form.prism_form_field_kind_str(field)
    // ⛔ EARLY RETURN PER BRANCH, never one `let x = if …` chain merging them. Each constructor
    // yields a Result whose payload carries a DIFFERENT PrismFormFieldKind shape, and merging
    // those heterogeneous branches into a single binding produced a value that faulted when read
    // back later (an access violation inside encode_values, only for a field rebuilt this way).
    // Same mechanism as the dangling-`let =` defect fixed above, generalised from strings to enum
    // payloads. `_prism_fm_finish` carries the rules/help re-attachment so the branches stay
    // one-liners and the shared tail exists exactly once.
    if kind == "text"
        return _prism_fm_finish(field, prism_ui_form.prism_form_text(name, label, raw)?)
    if kind == "email"
        return _prism_fm_finish(field, prism_ui_form.prism_form_email(name, label, raw)?)
    if kind == "password"
        return _prism_fm_finish(field, prism_ui_form.prism_form_password(name, label, raw)?)
    if kind == "number"
        return _prism_fm_finish(field, prism_ui_form.prism_form_number(name, label, parse_int_safe(raw)?, prism_ui_form.prism_form_field_number_min(field, 0), prism_ui_form.prism_form_field_number_max(field, 0))?)
    if kind == "range"
        return _prism_fm_finish(field, prism_ui_form.prism_form_range(name, label, parse_int_safe(raw)?, prism_ui_form.prism_form_field_range_min(field, 0), prism_ui_form.prism_form_field_range_max(field, 0), prism_ui_form.prism_form_field_range_step(field, 1))?)
    if kind == "choice"
        return _prism_fm_finish(field, prism_ui_form.prism_form_choice(name, label, prism_ui_form.prism_form_field_choice_options(field), raw)?)
    if kind == "toggle"
        return _prism_fm_finish(field, prism_ui_form.prism_form_toggle(name, label, _prism_fm_parse_bool(raw)?)?)
    err("prism_form: field '{name}' has an unrecognized kind '{kind}' -- prism_ui_form.nova's vocabulary grew since this file was written; add a case to _prism_fm_apply_raw")

// The shared tail: reattach the ORIGINAL field's rules and help, which prism_ui_form's
// constructors reset. Exists so every branch above stays a single expression.
fn _prism_fm_finish(orig: PrismFormField, fresh: PrismFormField) -> Result<PrismFormField>
    let rules = prism_ui_form.prism_form_field_rules(orig)
    let with_rules = prism_ui_form.prism_form_field_with_rules(fresh, rules)?
    let help = prism_ui_form.prism_form_field_help(orig)
    ok(prism_ui_form.prism_form_field_with_help(with_rules, help))

// Interprets `raw` per the NAMED field's current kind and returns a new state with that ONE field
// updated -- `fm_st_saved`/`fm_st_version` are untouched (editing is not saving). Errs if no field
// named `fname` exists, or if `raw` does not parse for that field's kind (an out-of-range Range
// value, a non-integer Number/text, a Choice value naming no real option, an unrecognized boolean).
fn prism_fm_set_field(state: PrismFmState, fname: string, raw: string) -> Result<PrismFmState>
    let fields = prism_ui_form.prism_form_fields(state.fm_st_form)
    // ⛔ An INT cell, not `[false]`. A NOVA bool cannot be type-validated and its representation
    // changes across a call boundary, so a bool parked in a list cell (an `any` slot) and read
    // back is exactly the documented hazard -- here it faulted with an access violation.
    let found = [0]
    let out = []
    for field in fields
        if prism_ui_form.prism_form_field_name(field) == fname
            let updated = _prism_fm_apply_raw(field, raw)?
            push(out, updated)
            found[0] = 1
        else
            push(out, field)
    if found[0] == 0
        return err("prism_form: no field named '{fname}' on this form")
    let new_form = prism_ui_form.prism_form(out)?
    ok(PrismFmState(new_form, state.fm_st_saved, state.fm_st_version))

// The current values become the new baseline (e.g. after a successful `prism_fm_submit` and a
// confirmed server write) -- NOT called automatically by `prism_fm_submit` itself, because this
// module cannot observe whether the caller's own network/DB call actually succeeded. Recomputes
// `fm_st_version` so a stale draft tied to the OLD baseline correctly conflicts against this one.
fn prism_fm_mark_saved(state: PrismFmState) -> Result<PrismFmState>
    let version = _prism_fm_fingerprint(state.fm_st_form)?
    ok(PrismFmState(state.fm_st_form, state.fm_st_form, version))

// ── Accessors ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

fn prism_fm_current_form(state: PrismFmState) -> PrismForm
    state.fm_st_form

fn prism_fm_saved_form(state: PrismFmState) -> PrismForm
    state.fm_st_saved

fn prism_fm_version(state: PrismFmState) -> string
    state.fm_st_version

// ── Dirty tracking ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Includes secret fields deliberately (a password EDIT must still count as "unsaved changes" even
// though its value can never be autosaved -- see SECRETS) -- this stays entirely in memory, never
// persisted, so including it here does not weaken the draft-store guarantee at all.
fn prism_fm_is_dirty(state: PrismFmState) -> Result<bool>
    let cur = _prism_fm_encode_values(state.fm_st_form, 1)?
    let saved = _prism_fm_encode_values(state.fm_st_saved, 1)?
    if len(cur) != len(saved)
        return ok(true)
    for k, v in cur
        if not (k in saved)
            return ok(true)
        if saved[k] != v
            return ok(true)
    ok(false)

// ── Submit ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Total: either every field's validated raw value (a dict, name -> string, INCLUDING secrets -- see
// SECRETS on why that is the correct, deliberate exception), or every reason submission is refused,
// joined into one message naming each offending field. Reuses prism_ui_form.prism_form_validate's
// OWN rules -- never a second, divergent notion of "valid" from the one the rendered form shows
// errors for.
fn prism_fm_submit(state: PrismFmState) -> Result<dict>
    let errs = prism_ui_form.prism_form_validate(state.fm_st_form)?
    if len(errs) > 0
        let msgs = []
        for e in errs
            let field_name = prism_ui_form.prism_form_error_field(e)
            let message = prism_ui_form.prism_form_error_message(e)
            push(msgs, "{field_name}: {message}")
        let joined = join(msgs, "; ")
        let n = len(errs)
        return err("prism_form: cannot submit -- {n} field(s) invalid: {joined}")
    _prism_fm_encode_values(state.fm_st_form, 1)

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ── Draft / autosave (#87) -- this module never touches storage, see design decision ───────────

// {"v":"<sha256 of the saved baseline this draft was taken against>","f":{"<field>":"<raw value>",
// ...}} -- secret-kind fields are simply ABSENT from "f" (never a placeholder, never a masked
// marker -- see SECRETS). Hand-built via `+`/`json_stringify`, matching prism_secret.nova's own
// wire-form convention -- never the generic `to_json()` builtin.
fn prism_fm_draft_snapshot(state: PrismFmState) -> Result<string>
    let vals = _prism_fm_encode_values(state.fm_st_form, 0)?
    let f_json = _prism_fm_canonical_json(vals, state.fm_st_form)
    ok("\{\"v\":" + json_stringify(state.fm_st_version) + ",\"f\":" + f_json + "\}")

// Parses the envelope shape strictly (a snapshot that is not this module's own shape is a hard
// error -- it did not come from `prism_fm_draft_snapshot`, so nothing about its meaning can be
// assumed) but is lenient about individual field entries -- see `prism_fm_draft_apply`.
fn _prism_fm_decode_snapshot(snapshot: string) -> Result<list>
    let parsed = json_parse(snapshot)
    if type_of(parsed) != "dict"
        return err("prism_form: draft snapshot is not a JSON object")
    if not ("v" in parsed)
        return err("prism_form: draft snapshot is missing 'v' (version) -- not a value this module wrote")
    if not ("f" in parsed)
        return err("prism_form: draft snapshot is missing 'f' (fields) -- not a value this module wrote")
    let v = parsed["v"]
    let f = parsed["f"]
    if type_of(v) != "string"
        return err("prism_form: draft snapshot 'v' must be a string")
    if type_of(f) != "dict"
        return err("prism_form: draft snapshot 'f' must be an object")
    ok([v, f])

// See DESIGN DECISION "dirty is measured from a fingerprint..." for the FmDraftNone-outranks-
// FmDraftConflicting precedence: a draft that changes nothing is never worth a conflict prompt,
// even if the underlying record did technically move. `changed` -- field names the draft would
// actually change, relative to `current_vals` (the record's CURRENT saved, non-secret values) --
// is computed inline (single call site) rather than via a separate helper: a draft entry naming a
// field no longer present in `current_vals` (removed/renamed since the draft was taken) is compared
// against "" and so is reported changed (surfaced, not hidden) even though `prism_fm_draft_apply`
// will end up skipping it (see there).
fn prism_fm_draft_inspect(fresh: PrismFmState, snapshot: string) -> Result<PrismFmDraftInfo>
    let decoded = _prism_fm_decode_snapshot(snapshot)?
    let draft_version = decoded[0]
    let draft_fields = decoded[1]
    let current_vals = _prism_fm_encode_values(fresh.fm_st_saved, 0)?
    let changed = []
    for k, v in draft_fields
        if type_of(v) == "string"
            let cur = if k in current_vals
                current_vals[k]
            else
                ""
            if cur != v
                push(changed, k)
    let status = if len(changed) == 0
        FmDraftNone()
    else if draft_version == fresh.fm_st_version
        FmDraftRecoverable()
    else
        FmDraftConflicting()
    ok(PrismFmDraftInfo(status, changed))

// Applies a previously-inspected draft's field values onto `fresh` via `prism_fm_set_field` (the
// SAME function a live UI edit calls -- see the raw-string-setter design decision). Refuses a
// conflicting draft unless `allow_conflict` is explicitly true -- the CALLER decides whether to
// show a diff and let a human choose, or discard; this function only enforces that the refusal is
// the DEFAULT, never silent (see the CONFLICT design decision -- who decides). Best-effort per
// field: a draft entry that no longer parses against the field's CURRENT kind (the schema moved
// under it -- e.g. it held "hello" and the field is now typed as a number) is skipped rather than
// failing the whole recovery over one stale value; a draft entry naming a field that no longer
// exists is skipped the same way (`prism_fm_set_field` errors on an unknown name, which this loop
// treats as "nothing to apply", not a hard failure).
fn prism_fm_draft_apply(fresh: PrismFmState, snapshot: string, allow_conflict: bool) -> Result<PrismFmState>
    let info = prism_fm_draft_inspect(fresh, snapshot)?
    let conflicting = match info.fm_di_status
        FmDraftConflicting() => true
        FmDraftRecoverable() => false
        FmDraftNone() => false
    if conflicting and not allow_conflict
        return err("prism_form: draft conflicts with a newer saved version of this record -- refusing to apply automatically; pass allow_conflict=true only after the caller has resolved the conflict (e.g. shown the changed fields and the user chose to keep the draft anyway)")
    let decoded = _prism_fm_decode_snapshot(snapshot)?
    let draft_fields = decoded[1]
    let acc = [fresh]
    for k, v in draft_fields
        if type_of(v) == "string"
            let applied = prism_fm_set_field(acc[0], k, v)
            if not is_err(applied)
                acc[0] = unwrap(applied)
    ok(acc[0])

// Coarse "is there anything worth asking the user about at all" -- both Recoverable and
// Conflicting warrant SOME prompt (a plain "restore your draft?" for the former, a more careful
// diff-and-confirm for the latter, distinguishable via `prism_fm_draft_status`); FmDraftNone means
// the draft is indistinguishable from the current saved state, so offering recovery would be noise
// -- the exact failure mode #87's brief calls out.
fn prism_fm_should_offer_recovery(info: PrismFmDraftInfo) -> bool
    match info.fm_di_status
        FmDraftNone() => false
        FmDraftRecoverable() => true
        FmDraftConflicting() => true

fn prism_fm_draft_status(info: PrismFmDraftInfo) -> PrismFmDraftStatus
    info.fm_di_status

fn prism_fm_draft_status_str(info: PrismFmDraftInfo) -> string
    match info.fm_di_status
        FmDraftNone() => "none"
        FmDraftRecoverable() => "recoverable"
        FmDraftConflicting() => "conflicting"

fn prism_fm_draft_changed_fields(info: PrismFmDraftInfo) -> list
    info.fm_di_changed_fields
